## The Core Observation

Current AI architectures are built around one fundamental operation:

```
Matrix multiplication
    → Float32 / Float16 / Int8
    → Runs on GPU tensor cores
    → Hardware costs $200 — $10,000+
    → Draws 150W — 700W
    → Designed for this exact purpose
```

Cryptographic hashing silicon is built around different fundamental operations:

```
Hash function computation
    → Fixed-size input → fixed-size output
    → Runs on dedicated ASIC logic
    → Hardware costs $15 — $500 used
    → Draws 5W — 233W
    → Designed for cryptographic proof-of-work
```

The question this document asks:

**Is there a class of neural network architecture where the fundamental operations align with what hashing silicon does natively — making that silicon a viable inference platform?**

This is not a rhetorical question. It is an open research question with a non-trivial answer.

-----

## Why Hashing Silicon Exists

Mining ASICs exist because the market paid billions of dollars to build the most efficient possible implementations of specific mathematical functions. The result is silicon that performs those functions at a cost-per-operation orders of magnitude below what a GPU or CPU can achieve.

```
Scrypt on CPU:        ~50,000 H/s       at ~65W
Scrypt on GPU:        ~10,000,000 H/s   at ~150W
Scrypt on ASIC:       185,000,000 H/s   at ~233W

Cost per hash:
CPU:   0.0013 W per 1000 H/s
GPU:   0.015  W per 1000 H/s
ASIC:  0.0013 W per 1000 H/s  ← same efficiency as CPU but 3700x faster
```

That efficiency exists because the silicon does exactly one thing. The question is whether that one thing — or a close variant of it — is useful for AI inference.

