## Hash-Native Neural Architecture — Concept

### What a Standard Architecture Looks Like

```
Input
    ↓ learned embedding (matrix multiply)
Token representations
    ↓ attention (matrix multiply + softmax)
Contextualized representations
    ↓ feed-forward (matrix multiply + activation)
Output logits
    ↓ softmax
Probability distribution
```

Every arrow is a matrix multiplication. The hardware requirement is GPU tensor cores.

### What a Hash-Native Architecture Could Look Like

```
Input
    ↓ hash-based feature encoding
      (fixed projection via hardware hash — deterministic, no learned weights)
Hash-encoded features
    ↓ LSH-based sparse attention
      (hardware LSH finds relevant tokens — no full attention matrix needed)
Sparse context set
    ↓ hash-accumulator layers
      (hash composition as learned transform — runs on ASIC natively)
Accumulated representation
    ↓ proof-of-work verified output
      (output carries PoW token — verifiable computation provenance)
Output with hardware attestation
```

### Key Differences

|Property             |Standard Transformer|Hash-Native Architecture           |
|---------------------|--------------------|-----------------------------------|
|Core operation       |Matrix multiply     |Hash composition                   |
|Hardware target      |GPU tensor cores    |Cryptographic ASIC                 |
|Hardware cost        |$200–$10,000+       |$15–$500 used                      |
|Power draw           |150–700W            |5–65W                              |
|Output provenance    |None                |Cryptographic — hardware-verifiable|
|Attention mechanism  |Full O(n²)          |Sparse LSH-based                   |
|Weight representation|Float32/16          |Hash-space embeddings              |
|Inference speed      |Fast on GPU         |Fast on ASIC for its operations    |
|Portability          |GPU required        |Any compatible ASIC                |

