## Mathematical Foundations

### Hash Functions as Neural Primitives

A hash function H maps input x to output H(x) with these properties:

- Deterministic
- Fixed output size
- Avalanche effect (small input change → large output change)
- One-way (cannot reverse)

These properties have analogues in neural network operations:

```
Deterministic       → reproducible inference
Fixed output size   → fixed-dimension embeddings
Avalanche effect    → sensitive feature detection
One-way             → information compression (encoder behavior)
```

### LSH as Sparse Attention

Locality-Sensitive Hashing (LSH) finds similar items without comparing all pairs. Applied to attention:

```
Standard attention:   compare every token to every other token O(n²)
LSH attention:        hash tokens into buckets, attend within buckets O(n log n)
```

Reformer (Kitaev et al., 2020) demonstrated LSH attention works for transformers. Hash-native architecture extends this — the LSH computation runs on dedicated hashing silicon rather than GPU.

### Scrypt’s Salsa20/8 Core

Scrypt’s internal mixing function uses Salsa20/8 — a stream cipher operating on 512-bit blocks with 8 rounds of ARX operations (Add, Rotate, XOR).

ARX operations on fixed-size blocks have structural similarity to:

- Attention mixing operations
- Feed-forward layer operations
- Normalization operations

Whether this similarity is deep enough to support useful neural computation is the core mathematical question this research direction must answer.

